## Achieving Morphological Control over Lamellar Manganese Metal-Organic Framework through Modulated Bi-phase Growth
Dr. Y. Shen, Dr. B. Shan, Prof. B. Mu, and Prof. S. Tongay
School for Engineering of Matter, Transport and Energy, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ 85287, USA
\* Email: bmu@asu.edu (Bin Mu) and sefaattin.tongay@asu.edu (Sefaattin Tongay)
Abstract
Metal-organic framework (MOF) materials offer wealth of applications depending on if they are crystallized in bulk or layered nature. However, the control over this morphology is difficult especially for those involving metal cations with low electronegativity and uncontrollable kinetic reaction. Here, we report modulated biphase synthesis route towards large-scale manganese 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (MnBDC) with a precise control over their morphology i.e., bulk vs layered. In this bi-phase method, metal precursors and organic ligands are separated to reduce the kinetic reaction rates for better control over the crystallization process. Based on scanning electron microscope (SEM), X-ray diffraction (XRD), energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) and Raman spectroscopy studies, we find that the continuous ligand supply along with the presence of pyridine capping agent are highly effective in promoting the layer-by-layer growth and achieving large crystal sizes. Once layered MnBDC is stabilized, we further used topotactic intercalation chemistry to demonstrate the feasibility of bromine intercalation on these layered materials. Comprehensive studies show that bromine intercalation is possible between the MOFs layers for the first time. Interestingly, bromine intercalation causes colossal reduction in layered MnBDC band gap while it has no observable effect on bulk MOFs. Overall results
