these studies, it has been shown that the key factor in achieving layered mBDC layers relies on the use of carefully engineered ligands to terminate the out-of-plane sites on metal clusters thereby eliminating strong interlayer hydrogen bond formation. Cu and Zn based mBDCs are relatively easy to work with since i) their electronegativity values are relatively high and ii) their coordination number take four or five which is convenient to tile them in a periodic fashion on 2Ds. In contrast, the coordination number of Mn is six which gives additional degrees of freedom and thus introduces added complexities in the control over morphology and structure.
In this work, we focus on this Mn based MOFs as a study case to investigate the growth characteristics of MnBDC MOFs. The ultimate goal is to develop potential growth routes and processing steps have a control over their morphology (bulk vs layered) as well as their physical lateral sizes. Our results show that it is possible to stabilize the layered form of MnBDC through pyridine capping agent when coupled with the proposed bi-phase growth system (Figure 1a). Once stabilized in the layered form, we further treat lamellar MnBDC MOFs using bromine gas to enable intercalation chemistry (topotactic reaction) for improved electronic conductivity via bromine (Br) intercalation for the first time. Overall results introduce novel manufacturing concepts to enable layered MOFs containing challenging metal cations and open up new ways to modify material properties through intercalation chemistry techniques.
