3D bulk MnBDC through conventional one-pot technique  In the process of MOF crystal formation, the relative interaction between metal cations and organic carboxylate linkers mainly determines the crystallization rate and thus production quality as shown for variety of different 3D MOFs in the past [8]. One established example is Ti-MOFs in which strong interaction formed by Ti-carboxylate bonds hinders the reversible association $/$ dissociation from which the structural defects can be restored [6b]. Possessing a similar electronegativity as Ti (1.54), Mn (1.55) inevitably has the similar difficulties in its crystallization process. In fact, by using conventional ‘one-pot’ growth method [9], we have mixed $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ and carboxylate solutions together (see Experimental Section) to form Mn-BDC materials. In this one-pot method, typical growth process had high chemical reaction rates and produced instant gel-like products upon mixture (Figure S1). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) studies on these MnBDC show that the products appear in a powder form in which each single crystal domains assume rhombic (or cubic) form with lateral sizes ranging from 1-5 microns as depicted in Figure 1b. These 3D bulk MnBDC have been experimentally realized in the past [9-10] and their crystallographic properties have been established (Figure 1c) [10]. Starting from the established crystal structure, we have simulated the XRD datasets from 5-40 degrees using CrystalMaker which is shown as black solid line in Figure 1c. We find that the measured XRD datasets from the synthesized MnDBCs XRD pattern (Figure 1c in red) closely matches to the simulated datasets consistent with the published results [10]. The most predominant diffraction peaks in these rhombic MnBDC originate from (-111) and (-222) reflections at $9.78^{\circ}$ and $19.58^{\circ}$ , as well as (112) at $18.64^{\circ}$ . The simulated structure (Figure 1d) indicates that the coordination number of Mn is six. Four of the Mn sites are connected to carboxylate ligand and the other two bond with N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) solvent molecules (cyan circle in Figure 1d) to form 3D structure.
