Modulated bi-phase synthesis to stabilize layered MnBDC  In order to enable layered crystallization, we discuss our bi-phase growth system in which capping agent pyridine ensures the loose stacking of layers. As schematically illustrated in Figure 1a ‘Modulated Bi-Phase’, the top phase consists of Mn cations and capping agent pyridine dissolved in DMF. While, the bottom phase contains chloroform with dispersed 1,4- benzenedicarboxylate (BDC) ligand. Then, drop DMF solution carefully at the chloroform surface avoiding any serious perturbation until forming a continuous top layer. After transferring the bi-phase system, the crystallization occurred in the autoclave at $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 24 hours. In this bi-phase arrangement, metal and ligand precursors are physically separated from each other by 2D interface between them. This allows us to kinetically control the crystallization process by forming concentration gradients of reactants. Since BDC ligands have a better solubility in DMF, this drives BDC to gradually diffuse toward the upper phase and meet Mn cations (blue ball) around the intermediate region. Notably, such diffusion process ensures MnBDC are crystallized locally where ligand is in highly diluted. (the white regions with blue frames shown in Figure 1a in ‘Modulated Bi-Phase’ situations).
