Apart from the diffusion mechanism mentioned above, the capping agent (pyridine) used in the system further promotes the morphology (Figure 1a, in ‘Modulated Bi-Phase’). The ultimate goal of using pyridine is to terminate the out-of-plane metal sites (or reduce the available bonding) to better tile them in 2Ds and also to minimize the interlayer coupling strength to achieve layer-by-layer vdW stacking. Our results show that the addition of pyridine agent in the upper phase (mixed with metal cations) is effective in producing highly lamellar MnBDC as shown in Figure 2a-b. The termination effect of pyridine is consistent with our previous report [7].
To understand the crystal structure of the synthesized layered MnBDC, we refined the simulated unit cell and compared the simulated XRD to the experimental datasets collected from lamellar MnBDC in Figure 2c. X-ray diffraction of the solid product showed limited reflections which can be indexed as the (100), (010), (200), (020) and (300) at $\boldsymbol{10.08^{\circ}}$ , $10.63^{\circ}$ , $\boldsymbol{18.70^{\circ}}$ , $21.19^{\circ}$ and $28.16^{\circ}$ respectively. None of the additional Bragg diffractions of the rhombic counterpart were detected. These intense diffractions indicate a strong preferential orientation, and lattice are stacking along (100) and $(010)$ directions, corresponding to a spacing distance of 9.5 and $8.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Furthermore, we applied Raman vibration to depict the unit cell structure of lamellar MnBDC (Figure 2d). Excited by incident laser with a wavelength of $488~\mathsf{n m}$ , obtained lamellar MnBDC crystal shows four dominant Raman peaks $({\mathsf{P}}_{1}\sim{\mathsf{P}}_{4})$ in the range of $700~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ to $1700~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ which are assigned as the vibrations of metal cluster breathing, benzene ring breathing, metal cluster contracting and benzene ring breathing, respectively [7]. These Raman features collected by attained lamellar MnBDC precisely match with previous results collected from other vdW 2D MOFs containing Zn or Cu metal atoms [7]. Notably, we found that these four featured peaks in lamellar MnBDC uniformly show their full width at half maximum (FWHM) as narrow as $6.1\sim6.8~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . Such narrow FWHM in Raman peaks indicates high crystallinity of products, which is comparative with classic inorganic crystals, like ${\mathsf{M o S}}_{2}$ intensely constructed via strong covalent bonds [11].
