Effects of bi-phase design and capping agent on morphology and orientation  Two series of control experiments were designed to understand how the reactants diffusion control and the type of the capping agent influence the crystal growth characteristics and the structure of the material: In the first set of series (Series-I), pyridine capping agent was utilized in single-phase; whereas, in the second set of series (SeriesII) only bi-phase system was created without pyridine. Typical SEM image collected from samples produced by Series-I experiments is shown in Figure 3a. When pyridine is added directly in single-phase solution, produced MnBDC crystals show thin flaky morphology and some sign of regular edges with hexagonal shapes. As expected, MnBDC grown in this case exhibit similar XRD pattern (purple pattern in Figure 3c) to simulated lamellar data discussed above wherein (100) and (010) are the two mostly predominant peaks (remarked as starts). The morphological difference to established one-pot product confirmed our previous hypothesis about the termination effect of pyridine. The occupation of pyridine on Mn out-of-plane sites promotes the crystallization along xy basal plane. However, the diffraction pattern contains many additional rough peaks
