which suggests the relatively low crystallinity. We argue that this effect is related to the fast reaction speeds in the one-phase system which allows all the precursors to react without any minute control. In the case of single-phase, MnBDC crystals form by fast reaction between Mn cations and carboxylate ligands caused by the absence of concentration control.
In Series-II, only bi-phase is applied without pyridine. Typical morphologies characterized by SEM are shown in Figure 3b. In this growth, synthesized crystals take hexagonal shape, instead of rhombus observed in ‘one-pot’ MnBDC.  More importantly, it is clearly seen that crystals obtained in bi-phase system have lateral size in hundreds’ micron range, much larger than those in single-phase counterparts. We attribute this result as a kinetics governed crystallization realized via diffusion process. Distinct from single-phase, metal and ligand in bi-phase system are separated in two liquid phases. Chloroform (bottom phase) has limited solubility to H2BDC ligand even after sonication. When the bottom phase is covered by metal solution (DMF solvent), ligand spread into the interfacial region first, and then move upward to metal phase gradually. Crystallization occurs only when ligand meet with metal driven by its diffusion process (depicted in Figure 3d). As such, a concentration gradient of ligand leads to a gradually increased ligand supply which would benefit a good crystallization. XRD result (Figure 3c) of Series-II (bi-phase without pyridine) exhibits a similar pattern to lamellar MnBDC demonstrated in Figure 2c. Their predominant diffractions at (100) and (010) planes suggest that products in Series-II are crystallized favorably along in-plane direction. Such mechanism of preferred orientation resulted from diffusion process or liquid-liquid interface were mentioned in the synthesis of layered CuBDC and NAFS-1[12].
