We find that near the position at the bottom of glass vial (spot 1), when the concentration of ligands is oversaturated, large number of small nucleates and crystallites form. Moving from spot $1\rightarrow2$ , the concentration of ligands (denoted as $"c(1)\%"$ in Figure 4b) decreases and simultaneously the density of nucleates decreases and the synthesized MnBDC size become larger (spot 2). Moving along spots $3\substack{\rightarrow6}$ similar trends can be observed wherein small crystallites (nucleates) slowly disappear and crystal sizes become larger for decreasing ligand concentration. Nanoscale Raman spectroscopy has been applied to characterize crystal structure and crystallinity of nucleates and crystals as shown in Figure S4. As-grown MnBDC crystals exhibit consistent Raman peaks $({\mathsf{P}}_{1}\sim{\mathsf{P}}_{4})$ to the results we mentioned in Figure 2d. While, the rough curve and especially for the broaden peaks of $\mathsf{P}_{3}$ and $\mathsf{P}_{4}$ in the spectrum of nucleates sample indicated its poor crystallinity. The spatial distribution of MnBDC crystallization confirmed our statement regarding to the diffusion governed crystallization. It evident that in our designed bi-phase system, the low c $(1)\%$ (dilute) favors the crystallization process. Based on our observations, it is apparent that MnBDC crystallization does not only occurs at the intermediate zone, but half of the metal ion rich zones are more favorable to grow MnBDC crystals. These long-lasting supply results to the formation of sub-millimeter massive crystals. However, these large size crystals grown through bi-phase method (without any modulator) exhibit 3D bulky feature. There is no significant lamellar morphology observed. We attribute this phenomenon to the naturally out-of-plane bonding of Mn clusters with DMF molecules owing to its coordination number of six, occurred similarly to the pristine MnBDC grown in conventional method. In a contrast, these metal sites are occupied by capping agent and resulting loose stacking in the modulated bi-phase system with the existence of pyridine.
Bromine intercalation   Before discussing intercalation chemistry on layered MnBDC, we first discuss the fundamental material properties of layered sheets and bulk MnBDC (via bi-phase method without pyridine, its morphology is displayed in Figure 3b). To probe their optical properties, we have performed diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (DRS) measurements to understand the differences in the optical properties of 3D bulk and lamellar MnBDC crystals. DRS results of bulk and lamellar crystals were collected with the photon energy in the range of ultraviolet-visible-near IR. The optical band gap of these two samples were estimated from Kubelka-Munk Function based on measured DRS results (Figure 5a and 5b). Our results show that the pristine
