lamellar MnBDC has an optical band gap of $3.79\mathsf{e V}$ while for bulk MnBDC is around 2.90 eV. In fact, MnBDC bulk crystals appear brownish in color, lamellar ones are almost white (Figure S2a) due to increase in the gap values.
Figure 5. Optical properties and $\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ -interaction behavior conducted in lamellar and bulk MnBDC. a. and b., Tauc plots of lamellar (a.) and bulk MnBDC (b.) before and after $\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ treatment; c. Scanning Electron Microscopy of typical morphology of lamellar MnBDC after $\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ intercalation; d. elemental compositions detected from lamellar MnBDC, with Br elemental ratios denoted; e. schematic of Br interaction mechanism of lamellar MnBDC.
Now that layered MnBDC MOFs are stabilized, we explore the possibility of band engineering using intercalation chemistry technique. This topotactic method has been heavily used to change the band gap as well as electronic conductivity of vdW layered inorganic materials such as graphite/graphene and transition metal dichalcogenides [14]. However, this method is not so straightforward for MOFs or other polymeric materials owing to their strong interlayer coupling [15]. During the $\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ treatment experiments (details see Figure S2), lamellar and bulk MnBDC crystals were spread flat at the bottom of two glass plates separately as shown
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
in the schematic diagram (Figure 5a). All the experiments were performed in a chemical fume hood with proper personal safety. First, bromine liquid (2 mL) was dropped in another glass container and inserted into large beaker with its lid open. Both bulk and lamellar materials were placed in this beaker and sealed using parafilm to expose bromine gas to materials. Because bromine has a high vapor pressure at room temperature, the bromine gas reached its saturated vapor pressure within 30 minutes and the entire sealed beaker gradually fills up with gas from light orange to dark red (illustrated by the schematic inserted in Figure 5a). Once the bromine treatment was completed (three hours), MOFs were collected and characterized immediately.
The DRS comparison before and after the bromine treatment is shown in Figure 5a-b. Interestingly, bulk MOFs DRS spectra remain unchanged meaning the material remains the same without any change in the material properties. In contrast, layered MnBDC exhibited large red-shift in their band gap from 3.79 eV to 2.04 eV (Figure 5a). We attribute large band gap variation to the layered nature of our MnBDC sheets enabling bromine gas to intercalate between the layers. However, the fundamental origin of such red-shift in the optical gap still remains unknown due to complexities in calculating the electronic band structure of Br intercalated MnBDC layers. We argue that the intercalated Br atoms must have an overall effect to increase the energy of valance band maxima (VBM) or reduced the energy of conduction band minima (CBM) level to cause red-shift in the band gap. From SEM images, it is evident that the layered nature is retained after bromine intercalation suggesting that bromine exposure does not destroy the crystal structure (Figure 5c). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) points towards the elemental composition of MnBDC after Br intercalation and large Br presence as high as ${\sim}14.2$ at. $\%$ (denoted in Figure 5d). We note that the measure XRD pattern also clearly demonstrate the bromine intercalation without any staging phenomena. The set of diffraction peaks of (h00) with $h=1,2,3$ in pristine lamellar crystals all moved to smaller degrees after Br intercalation (as marked with stars in Figure S3). Correspondingly, the interlayer spacing is enlarged from $9.5\mathsf{A}$ to $10.4\mathsf{A}$ calculated from Bragg equation. As a comparison, the XRD patterns collected from bulk samples before/after $\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ treatment are remained largely unchanged. The proposed mechanism of lamellar MnBDC intercalation is illustrated in Figure 5e. While the nature of large redshift in their band gap values remain unknown but merit future studies,
