our results clearly demonstrate that the layered morphology facilitates the diffusion of bromine between MnBDC layers but the same phenomena is absent in their bulk counterparts.
Conclusion We have demonstrated a modulated bi-phase method for the synthesis of layered Mn MOFs with submillimeter scale of lateral size for the first time. By spatially separating metal and ligand in two liquid phases, the MOF crystallization is slowed down by the concentration gradient of ligand along vertical direction. The continuous ligand supply leads to the formation of large size crystals significantly. At the same time, addition of capping agent- pyridine has been identified as the primary factor to promote in-plane growth and loosely stacked of layers. The morphology and crystallinity have been characterized by SEM, XRD, EDS and Raman spectroscopy. Owing to large lateral size, layer-by-layer stacking, and high crystallinity, we were able to utilize bromine intercalation to investigate the changes in the band gap with intercalation chemistry. These findings offer a simple method to have precise control over morphology in microstructure and crystallinity of 2D layered MOFs.
